{"id": "ccb6eab6-1c00-44fd-8bd6-28572c43b2fd", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:50:07.649074", "source": "user", "parent_id": "c4fde81e-cd35-4134-9465-728c53153747", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Before we commit to a plan, please do a lightweight inspection pass only — no full design yet.\\n\\n1) Input/repository inspection: report what is actually available in /workspace (any repos, quantization code such as QuaRot/RTN/GPTQ implementations, model definitions, calibration/evaluation harnesses, datasets, and environment constraints). Note explicitly what is absent, since this is a design-only task and we should not assume compute, pretrained checkpoints, or benchmark data exist. Also skim the provided track.md framing to confirm the design-only scope.\\n\\n2) Proposed approach outline: sketch, at a high level, how you would structure an experiment design that isolates the effect of QuaRot's Hadamard rotation on weight-only low-bit quantization (W ∈ {4,3,2}), with RTN and GPTQ as baselines. Indicate the design axes you would vary (bit-width, rotation placement, quantization granularity/group size, outlier-handling ablations, model families/sizes, calibration data, evaluation metrics including downstream accuracy and perplexity) and how you would attribute gains specifically to the rotation rather than to confounds. Keep this as an outline: rationale, controls, analysis plan and feasibility notes will be the deliverable later.\\n\\n3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties: list the assumptions you would need to make (e.g. rotation applied to both weights and activations conceptually vs. weight-only effect, group-size choices at 2-bit, whether activation rotation is required for mathematical equivalence, which models/scales are realistic to target, calibration set size) and any place where the source material does not actually support a choice. Flag anything you consider unknown rather than guessing.\\n\\nDo not produce the final experiment design in this turn.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"How does the inclusion of QuaRot's Hadamard transformation improve the quality of weight-only quantized models under low-precision settings? Can QuaRot stabilize and improve performance at 2–4 bit weight precision where traditional quantization methods like RTN and GPTQ often fail?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 224, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [\"neurips2024/94328/4:setup_5\", \"neurips2024/94328/4:setup_7\", \"neurips2024/94328/4:setup_11\", \"neurips2024/94328/4:setup_16\", \"neurips2024/94328/4:setup_17\"], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
