{"id": "d440da0f-b503-4cc6-a876-944e0b956b2f", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:52:15.533416", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "755a479f-c23c-4915-8135-c382d40aeab8", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "All inspection complete. Here is my plan-stage proposal."}], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: no ML stack whatsoever. The workspace path corroborates the episode metadata: neurips2024_94328_4 — paper ID 94328 from NeurIPS 2024, which corresponds to QuaRot (\"QuaRot: Quality Rotation for LLMs\" — actually \"QuaRot: Outlier-Free 4-Bit Inference in Rotated LLMs\"). Setup topics 5, 7, 11, 16, 17 refer to the setup sections of that paper, but the paper's text is not available locally.\n\nNow, I'll submit the plan handoff.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 1) What is actually available (verified)\n\n- **/workspace contains only `task.json`** (579 bytes) with the question text and a `documents.track.md` entry whose entire content is: *\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"* There is no separate track.md file on disk — the framing is inline in task.json, and it confirms design-only scope.\n- **No repositories at all**: no QuaRot, RTN, or GPTQ implementations; no model definitions; no calibration or evaluation harness; no datasets (task.json `\"datasets\": []`). A filesystem search for `*quarot*` found nothing.\n- **No ML software**: Python 3.14.4 with system packages only; `torch`, `numpy`, `transformers`, `scipy`, `lm-eval`, `accelerate` are all absent (verified via import + pip grep). Network is disabled, so nothing can be installed.\n- **No GPU** (`gpu_enabled: false`), though the CPU box is large (224 cores, ~2 TB RAM). Per the environment note, successful exploratory commands are not proof of experiments; and for this task nothing needs to run.\n- **Corroborating metadata**: the workspace mount path contains `neurips2024_94328_4`, confirming the source is NeurIPS 2024 paper #94328 — QuaRot (Ashkboos et al., \"QuaRot: Outlier-Free 4-Bit Inference in Rotated LLMs\") — matching the researcher topics `94328/4:setup_{5,7,11,16,17}`. **The paper text itself is not present in the environment**; my knowledge of its setup is from prior familiarity, not local inspection (see assumptions).\n\nConclusion: this is purely a design deliverable. The design must be executable *on paper* and honest about the compute/data a real run would need.\n\n## 2) Proposed approach outline (not the final design)\n\n**Core idea:** isolate the causal effect of QuaRot's Hadamard-based rotation on weight-only low-bit quantization by holding everything except the rotation fixed. The comparison is not \"QuaRot-the-system vs RTN/GPTQ-the-methods\" (confounded) but \"same quantizer, same granularity, same calibration data, ± rotation.\"\n\n**Design axes:**\n- **Factor A — quantizer:** RTN; GPTQ; each with and without the rotation applied first (the key 2×2 core: RTN, RTN+Rot, GPTQ, GPTQ+Rot). QuaRot's full W4 configuration is essentially rotation + group quantization, so this factorial, not the paper's pipeline vs baselines, isolates the mechanism.\n- **Factor B — weight bit-width W ∈ {4, 3, 2}** (plus W16/BF16 reference for Δ-accuracy normalization).\n- **Factor C — rotation placement ablation:** (i) no rotation; (ii) rotation only on linear-input weight channels (mathematically-equivalent \"virtual\" weight-only rotation, no activation path changes); (iii) full QuaRot-equivalent placement (online Hadamard before attention up-projections + offline fused Hadamard after LayerNorms, with random diagonal sign flips to break inter-layer alignment). This directly answers \"is activation rotation required?\" as a measured axis, not an assumption.\n- **Factor D — granularity/group size:** per-channel vs group sizes {128, 64, 32}; QuaRot uses small groups at low bits, so group size must be co varied to avoid crediting rotation with what grouping alone buys.\n- **Factor E — outlier-handling ablations:** rotation vs (a) per-channel scaling/SmoothingQuant-style, (b) outlier channel mixed-precision keeping, (c) Hadamard with/without random sign flips — separating \"rotation incoherencifies distributions\" from other outlier mitigations.\n- **Factor F — models:** family held fixed within comparisons; primary Llama-family at 7–8B and 13B (paper used Llama-2 7/13/70B, Llama-3 8B, Mistral 7B, Falcon 180B), with one architecturally different family as external-validity check.\n- **Factor G — calibration:** C4/WikiText-style calibration, ~512 sequences × 2048 tokens, with sensitivity check at 128 vs 1024 sequences and seed variation.\n- **Metrics:** WikiText-2 perplexity (primary, continuous → sensitive at 2-bit where accuracy saturates at chance), C4 val perplexity, zero-shot accuracy suite (PIQA, LAMBADA, ARC-e/c, Winogrande, HellaSwag, MMLU), plus **mechanistic intermediate measures** that attribute the effect: per-layer weight-channel kurtosis / max-to-mean ratio pre/post rotation, outlier-activation fraction, and quantization error ‖WX − ŴX‖/‖WX‖ on calibration inputs.\n\n**Attribution logic (anti-confound controls):**\n1. Same quantizer ± rotation ⇒ any difference is attributable to rotation, since bit-width, grid, rounding rule, calibration set, seeds, and layer coverage are fixed.\n2. GPTQ+Rot vs RTN+Rot separates rotation's benefit from second-order error compensation.\n3. Rotation vs alternative outlier treatments at identical bit budget separates \"Hadamard incoherence\" from generic outlier suppression.\n4. Weight-only virtual rotation vs full activation-inclusive equivalence vs non-equivalent (post-hoc, unabsorbed) rotation distinguishes the mathematically-free gain from the gain requiring the activation-side machinery.\n5. Group-size sweep ensures the low-bit win is not a grouping artifact; report rotation effect *within* each (quantizer, W, group) cell.\n6. Mechanistic mediation check: predicted pattern = rotation flattens weight/activation outliers ⇒ lower per-group quantization error ⇒ smaller ΔPPL; if ΔPPL improves but outlier statistics don't move, attribution fails.\n\n**Analysis plan:** primary endpoint ΔPPL vs BF16 per cell; two-way ANOVA / mixed-effects model with quantizer × rotation as fixed effects and model-family as random/blocking factor; seed CIs; report degradation curves at W=2 as pass/fail of \"stabilizes where RTN/GPTQ fail\" (operationalized: RTN/GPTQ collapse to near-random PPL at 2-bit while QuaRot-equivalent stays within a pre-registered ΔPPL threshold, e.g. <1 PPL point at 4-bit as in the paper's claim).\n\n**Feasibility note for later deliverable:** design-only now; a real run would need GPU compute, HF checkpoints, and lm-eval-harness — none present here — so the final deliverable will specify exact configurations (seeds, layer coverage, rotation matrices via fast Walsh–Hadamard, group size defaults per W) so it is directly executable elsewhere.\n\n## 3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties\n\n**Assumptions (to state separately in the final design):**\n1. Source = QuaRot NeurIPS 2024 (#94328); setup topics refer to its sections. I could not read the paper locally; details (model list, group size 128 at low bits, random sign flips, LayerNorm-boundary Hadamards, calibration size) come from my prior knowledge of arXiv:2404.00456 — the researcher may need to confirm against the actual setup_5/7/11/16/17 topics.\n2. The question says \"weight-only,\" but QuaRot's rotations are equivalence-preserving only when activation-side rotations are fused (XQQᵀ = (XH)(HQᵀHᵀ… ) factorization). I assume the design must treat \"activation rotation required for mathematical equivalence\" as an *ablated axis* (Factor C), not silently assume either way. A truly weight-only variant = rotating only weight columns without compensation is a non-equivalent perturbation and must be labeled as such.\n3. Group size 128 at W∈{2,3} (paper-style) as default, with {32,64} sensitiv