h a stated default and a sensitivity check, and mark them as unverified against the source. Do not attribute specific hyperparameters to the paper as fact. Likewise keep \\\"collapse\\\" and \\\"acceptable degradation\\\" thresholds as pre-registered parameters with a rationale, not as sourced numbers.\\n\\nFor the deliverable itself, please produce the final experiment design with: (1) rationale, centered on the 2x2 quantizer x rotation core and the anti-confound logic; (2) controls, including the group-size co-variation, the alternative outlier-treatment arms, seed/calibration-size sensitivity, and family-blocking; (3) analysis plan, including the primary endpoint, the mixed-effects/ANOVA structure, the pass/fail criterion operationalizing \\\"stabilizes at 2-3 bit where RTN/GPTQ fail,\\\" and the mediation check tying outlier-statistics movement to Delta-PPL (including what a null or dissociation result would mean); (4) feasibility, honestly scoped — what a real run would require (GPU, checkpoints, eval harness), rough cost drivers, and which axes to drop first if compute is limited, so the design degrades gracefully. Please state plainly that no experiment was run and none of the numbers in the design are measured results. Also flag the single biggest threat to validity in your own design rather than presenting it as airtight.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "7db5fbc6-c56b-4587-9c97-c64e2868bd5c", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:56:16.331164", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "a5bb7e71-b5cb-4573-9d94-10adb71ece6e", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Next, I'll create the full experimental design with stage=result. Comprehensive but organized. Incorporate the researcher's clarifications: weight-only as primary; RTN requires no calibration; knobs are parameterized; thresholds are pre-registered; feasibility is honest; single largest validity threat.\n\nLet me write carefully. No further tool calls needed — but should I run a quick sanity check? Not necessary. Submit the design.\n\nStructure:\n- Preamble statement: no experiments conducted, numbers are not measured.\n- 0. Restatement of fixed constraints.\n- 1. Rationale: causal question, 2×2 core, mechanism (incoherence/Hadamard), reason for choosing GPTQ.\n- 2. Design grid and factors: cells, parameterized knobs (group size g default 128 {32,64,128}; calibration size |C| default 512×2048 {128,1024}; rotation coverage; sign-flip on/off), outlier-handling arms, BF16 anchor, context arms.\n- 3. Controls and confound mitigation.\n- 4. Metrics and mechanistic measurements.\n- 5. Analysis plan: primary endpoint ΔPPL; mixed model; pass/fail criterion operationalization θ_fail, θ_ok; mediation analysis; interpretation of null/dissociation results.\n- 6. Feasibility: what's needed for execution, cost factors, ablation ordering.\n- 7. Assumptions vs. fixed requirements; largest validity threat.\n\nCell size estimate: quantizers {RTN, GPTQ} × rotation {none, weight-equivalent full fusion? wait — weight-only primary}.\n\nNote: \"weight-only\" quantization here means quantizing only weights (no activation quantization). The QuaRot-equivalent placement rotates the model function such that weights become more easily quantizable, and activations remain in BF16. Online Hadamard in attention is fused and… actually, QuaRot's online H is for A16 W4: applying an online Hadamard to activations of down_proj in attention, allowing V (or up projection weight) to be weight-only quantized. This is fine under weight-only: the model math is equivalent, activations remain in FP, but the rotation applied to activations at runtime is part of the equivalence machinery. Researcher says: Factor C's activation-inclusive placement is retained as a labeled equivalence reference/context arm; the primary arm is \"uncompensated weight-column-only rotation, labeled as non-equivalent\"? Wait, re-reading: \"Factor C's activation-inclusive placement is retained only as a clearly labeled equivalence reference/context arm, and uncompensated weight-column-only rotation is retained, labeled as a non-equivalent arm.\"\n\nHmm, that leaves the primary rotation arm ambiguous. Actually, in the weight-only case, a mathematically equivalent and practical rotation setup is: pre-multiply the input channels of the weight matrix by H (offline weight rotation, W' = W H) and fuse rotation of preceding layers — this is the offline equivalence rotation (embedding-boundary / LayerNorm fusion + per-block equivalence transformation) that stays entirely within the weight-only regime. The \"online\" Hadamard inside attention is for down_proj of value/out-projection, where the online activation transformation is needed — that's the activation-inclusive arm. And the uncompensated arm = rotating only a single weight matrix's columns without fusing compensating rotations into neighboring layers (changes the function — non-equivalent perturbation; useful as a control to show whether the gain comes from equivalence structure or just from rotating weights).\n\nSo the primary rotation arm (weight-only equivalence): offline fused Hadamard rotations: X = X (H Hᵀ); fuse XH into LN weight (W_ln H), rotate all linear input-side weights W ← W H; inter-block boundary: fuse to embedding and output; for attention's out-proj/o_proj whose inputs come from non-linear (softmax attention), an online or different rotation is required — hence the pure offline chain covers MLP and QKV projections, while o_proj/down_proj input rotations require the activation-inclusive arm. Good, this maps cleanly: primary arm = offline-equivalent rotation coverage (parameterized coverage knob); context arm = full QuaRot-equivalent including online Hadamard; non-equivalent arm = post-hoc weight-column-only rotation.\n\nPrimary grid: {RTN, GPTQ} × {W4,W3,W2} × {Rot: none, OFFLINE_EQ} = 12 cells per model × group size × seed. Additional arms: non-equivalent rotation × quantizer × bits (context), full equivalent (context) mainly at W2/W3, group-size sweep subset, outlier-handling arms (SmoothingQuant-style per-channel scaling, mixed-precision 0.1% outliers, sign-flip-off) subset, calibration sensitivity subset.\n\nAnalysis: mixed-effects model: ΔPPL ~ quantizer * rot + bit + g + (1|family/model) + (1|seed). 2×2 core at g=128.\n\nPass/fail criterion: define R_W = median ΔPPL for RTN/GPTQ without rotation; QuaRot-equivalent \"stabilizes at W\" if ΔPPL_rot ≤ θ_stab (default 2.0 points on WikiText-2? parameterize; reason: paper-scale Llama-2 7B PPL ~5.5, +2 ≈ 36% increase) AND baseline without rotation ≥ θ_fail (default 10 points or 5×, indicating collapse). Pre-registered parameters.\n\nMediation: per-layer channel kurtosis / max-RMS ratio of rotated vs. non-rotated weights, and relative quantization error on calibration inputs; test whether the rot→ΔPPL effect is attenuated when including quantization-error mediators; dissociation interpretation.\n\nFeasibility: per-cell GPTQ for 7B/13B on single A100 ~ minutes-to-hours; eval is the dominant cost; drop-order list.\n\nLargest validity threat: state it plainly. My pick: single-metric ΔPPL on WikiText-2 may not capture downstream quality at 2-bit, but a bigger structural threat is: the rotation arm's effect may be entangled with group quantization interaction — no, actually the biggest is the possibility that any gain from equivalence rotation is reproduced by simpler weight-space transformations at the same group size, or the reverse — actually the biggest threat in my design: the non-equivalent arm confounds function change with quantization benefit; or: compute constraints restrict us to ≤13B models, whereas the claim \"where RTN/GPTQ fail\" is scale-dependent (paper claims larger models benefit; outlier severity grows with scale/depth), so evidence from 7–13B may not generalize to the regime the question targets. Choose scale-dependent generalization as the largest threat with mitigation. Or alternatively: the interaction confound between rotation and group-size choice. The researcher asks for \"the single largest validity threat in your own design.\" Present: the effect estimate of offline-equivalent rotation depends on group size, and our grid may only show rotation's benefit at the default g; mitigation via co-variation sweep; but scale (7–13B vs 70/180B) realism is bigger. Pick scale generalization as largest, mention group-size interaction as a strong second.\n\nAlso explicitly note: RTN is calibration-free; RTN uses no data at all; rotation applied to RTN cells changes the weights before rounding but uses no calibration data — this keeps RTN calibration-free while varying only the rotation. GPTQ uses a calibration set; across GPTQ cells only rotation varies.\n\nWrite the answer comprehensively but not bloated. No tool calls needed; submit directly.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design: Isolating the Effect of QuaRot's Hadamard Transformation on Weight-Only Low-Bit Quantization (W ∈ {4,3,2}) vs. RTN and GPTQ\n\n**Plain statement up front:** No experiment was run. Every number below is either a pre-registered design parameter (with rationale) or an illustrative order-of-magnitude cost estimate. Nothing here is a measured result. The environment (/workspace: task.json only; no repos, no torch/numpy, no datasets, no GPU, no network) supports design only, and this deliverable is design only.\n\n## 0. Fixed constraints (from researcher brief, not assumptions)\n\n- **F1.** Weight-only quantization: only model weights are quantized. W4A4KV4 activation/KV arms are **out of scope as primary results** (setup_5, setup_7).\n- **F2.** W ∈ {4, 3, 2} (setup_11); BF16 appears only as a normalization anchor, never as a bit-width cell.\n- **F3.** RTN = round-to-nearest, **calibration-free** (setup_16). GPTQ = error-minimizing quantization via Hessian approximation on calibration data (setup_17).\n- **F4.** Group size, calibration size, rotation layer coverage, and sign-flip usage are **parameterized knobs with defaults, marked UNVERIFIED against the paper** — neither party has the source text locally; no hyperparameter below is attributed to the paper as fact.\n- **F5.** \"Collapse\" and \"acceptable degradation\" thresholds are pre-registered parameters with stated rationale, not sourced numbers.\n\n## 1. Rationale — the 2×2 quantizer × rotation core\n\nThe question conflates two things: (a) QuaRot-the-pipeline beating RTN/GPTQ-the-pipelines, and (b) whether the Hadamard **rotation itself** is the causal ingredient that stabilizes low-bit weight quantization. (a) is confounded — the pipeline differs in granularity, outlier handling, and coverage simultaneously. So the design's primary comparison is a **2×2 factorial held fixed on every other dimension**:\n\n|  | Rotation: OFF | Rotation: ON (offline-equivalent) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **RTN** (calibration-free) | C1 | C2 |\n| **GPTQ** (Hessian-based) | C3 | C4 |\n\n× bit-width W ∈ {4,3,2} = **12 primary cells per (model, group size, seed)**.\n\nMechanistic hypothesis being tested: Hadamard rotation multiplies weight/activation distributions by a structured orthogonal matrix, spreading energy across channels (incoherence reduction), which shrinks the dynamic range *within each quantization group*, thereby reducing rounding error ‖WX − Q(W)X‖ at fixed bit budget. The 2×2 core attributes any Δ to rotation because, within each quantizer row: identical grid, rounding rule, group size, layer coverage, seeds, and (for GPTQ) identical calibration set — **rotation is the only thing varied**. For RTN, no calibration data enters either RTN cell (the rotated weights are rounded without data; rotation modifies the pre-rounding weights, not the algorithm's data access), so RTN remains calibration-free as defined by F3.\n\nSecondary contrasts the same grid yields:\n- **C4 − C2** = does GPTQ's error compensation still help after rotation removes the error structure it compensates for? (Predicted: interaction — rotation's marginal gain shrinks as quantizer sophistication grows, and grows as W shrinks.)\n- **Gain(W) slope**: is the rotation effect largest at W=2, where RTN/GPTQ are expected to fail? This operationalizes the second half of the research question.\n\n## 2. Arms, knobs, and full grid\n\n### 2.1 Rotation arms (Factor C)\n\n- **R0 — none.** Raw BF16 weights → quantizer.\n- **R1 — offline-equivalent rotation (PRIMARY \"ON\" arm).** Mathematically equivalent transformation implemented entirely in the weights: fuse H into LayerNorm weights and across residual streams (X = XHHᵀ trick) at block boundaries with a fixed seed; rotate offline the input channels of every linear layer whose input is covered by the propagated rotation (embeddings, QKV projections, MLP up/gate projections), W ← WH. Activations stay BF16 at all times. **Coverage of the offline chain is a knob K_cov ∈ {full-linear-subset (default), attention-only, MLP-only} (UNVERIFIED default).**\n- **R1x — uncompensated weight-column-only rotation (NON-EQUIVALENT control arm).** Multiply each linear weight's input columns by H with no compensation anywhere. This deliberately changes the function; it is labeled non-equivalent and reported separately. Purpose: test whether naive \"rotate the weights\" (function-breaking) trivially mimics the gain, and quantify what the equivalence machinery buys.\n- **R2 — full QuaRot-equivalent placement including online Hadamards (EQUIVALENCE-REFERENCE / CONTEXTUAL arm, labeled non-primary per F1).** Adds online (runtime-activation, still BF16) Hadamards so that inputs of attention out-proj and MLP down-proj are also rotation-covered. Kept out of the primary ANOVA (it changes runtime compute and violates strict weight-only interpretation of \"nothing but weights changes\"); used only to bound how much of the effect is unreachable under strict weight-only placement.\n- **R-sign — sign-flip ablation knob K_sign ∈ {random diagonal sign flip ON (default), OFF} (UNVERIFIED default)**, applied within R1. Tests whether randomizing sign flips (breaking inter-block Hadamard alignment) matters.\n\n### 2.2 Quantizer cells\n\n- **RTN:** symmetric uniform grid, min/max per group (or per-channel for g=∞), no data used.\n- **GPTQ:** Hessian approximation from calibration forward passes, Cholesky-ordered column-wise error compensation, same grid/group size as paired RTN cell, identical hyperparameters (block size, damping 1% default) across rotation states.\n- Optional exploratory (not primary): GPTQ with non-uniform grid — flagged because the paper's exact GPTQ variant is unknown (uncertainty preserved, not guessed).\n\n### 2.3 Parameterized knobs (defaults are design choices, not paper claims)\n\n| Knob | Default | Sensitivity check | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Group size g | 128 | {32, 64, 128, per-channel(g=∞)} | UNVERIFIED |\n| Calibration set | C4-style, 512 × 2048-token seqs, seed s_C | {128, 512, 1024} seqs; 2 draws of s_C | UNVERIFIED |\n| Rotation coverage K_cov | offline-full | ablations above | UNVERIFIED |\n| Sign flips K_sign | ON | OFF | UNVERIFIED |\n| Quant seeds | 3 | — | design choice |\n\n### 2.4 Alternative outlier-treatment arms (Factor E) — same bit budget, no Hadamard\n\nAt each W, paired with R0: (a) **per-channel scaling / SmoothQuant-style migration** adapted to weight-only (per-channel max-rescaling); (b) **mixed-precision outlier keeping**: top-0.1% highest-|w| channels per layer kept BF16 (matched to the approximate extra-bit budget of small groups). These isolate \"Hadamard incoherence\" from generic outlier mitigation, so the design cannot accidentally credit rotation with benefits any range-reduction trick would give.\n\n### 2.5 Models (Factor F), blocked by family\n\n- **Primary:** Llama-family 7–8B and 13B (two scales within one family → outlier severity and quantization tolerance vary with scale/depth).\n- **External-validity block:** one architecturally different family at ~7B (e.g., Mistral-class or Falcon-class; specific choice deferred to checkpoints available at run time).\n- **Optional scale extension (clearly marked, drop-first):** 70B-class.\n- BF16 anchor per model for Δ normalization (F2).\n\n### 2.6 Full grid size (primary + secondary), for feasibility math\n\nPrimary: 2 quantizers × 2 rotation × 3 bits × g=128 × 3 models × 3 seeds = **108 quantization runs** (each cheap relative to eval). Secondary arms (R1x, R2, sign-off, coverage, outlier treatments, group sweep, calibration sensitivity) ≈ **+70–90 runs**, most at W ∈ {2,3} only, using a fractional-factorial subset rather than full crossing.\n\n## 3. Metrics\n\n- **Primary endpoint:** ΔPPL = PPL(cell) − PPL(BF16 anchor) on WikiText-2 (fixed evaluation protocol: 2048-token contiguous, non-overlapping). Secondary: C4-validation PPL.\n- **Downstream:** zero-shot suite (PIQA, LAMBADA, ARC-e/c, Winogrande, HellaSwag, MMLU) — reported as mean-normalized accuracy vs. BF16. Chosen because at W=2 PPL is a continuous, non-saturating measure where accuracy metrics floor at chance and lose resolution.\n- **Mechanistic/mediation measures (this is what makes the design explanatory, not just comparative):**\n  - M1: per-layer input-channel kurtosis and max/RMS ratio of weights, pre- vs post-rotation (does H flatten the distribution as theory predicts?).\n  - M2: relative quantization error on held-out calibration inputs, ε_rel = ‖WX − Q(W)X‖_F/‖WX‖_F per layer, in the same forward pass structure as each cell.\n  - M3: activation outlier statistics (max-activation fraction) — reported for context even though activations are not quantized (relates R1 vs R2 gap to residual activation outliers).\n- **Overhead accounting:** rotation/quantization wall-clock; since activations remain BF16 and R1 adds zero inference-time ops (fully fused), runtime cost is identical across R0/R1 cells — an important \"free lunch\" audit.\n\n## 4. Controls and anti-confound logic (consolidated)\n\n1. **Rotation-is-the-only-delta within quantizer rows** — grids, group size, rounding, damping, layer coverage, seeds, and GPTQ calibration set byte-identical between R0/R1 cells. RTN uses no data in either row (F3).\n2. **Group-size co-variation** — the g ∈ {32,64,128,∞} sweep is crossed with ±rotation at W ∈ {2,3}; the rotation effect is estimated *within* each (quantizer, W, g) cell, so a rotation win cannot be a grouping artifact, and a \"grouping explains it\" critique is directly testable.\n3. **Alternative outlier treatments at matched bit budget** (§2.4) — separates Hadamard incoherence from generic range reduction.\n4. **Equivalence audit** — assert PPL(R1, BF16 weights unquantized, rotations applied) within ±0.01 of BF16 anchor before any quantized cell is trusted; R1x is explicitly exempt (it must fail this audit — that's its purpose).\n5. **Family/scale blocking** — model entered as a blocking factor; all cells run within each model, so family effects cannot masquerade as rotation effects.\n6. **Calibration and seed sensitivity** — rotation contrast re-estimated at |C| ∈ {128,1024} and across 3 quant seeds and 2 calibration draws; RTN (data-free) doubles as a check that seed/reporting variance from data is being attributed to GPTQ only.\n7. **Non-primary arms quarantined** — R2 results are labeled contextual and excluded from the primary model, honoring F1.\n\n## 5. Analysis plan\n\n- **Primary endpoint:** ΔPPL_W2 for the second question; full ΔPPL surface for the first.\n- **Model:** mixed-effects regression\n  ΔPPL ~ quantizer × rotation × bits + (bits as factor) + g + (1 | model) + (1 | seed),\n  fitted on the primary 108 cells; planned contrasts (Tukey-adjusted): C2−C1, C4−C3, and interaction quantizer×rotation at each W. Family-blocking via model random effects; sensitivity refit with model as fixed effect.\n- **Pre-registered pass/fail criterion operationalizing \"stabilizes at 2–3 bit where RTN/GPTQ fail.\"** Two thresholds, declared before any run (F5 rationale, not sourced):\n  - θ_collapse = 10.0 ΔPPL (rationale: Llama-class 7–13B BF16 WikiText-2 PPL is single-digit; +10 is a >2× degradation and near-task-uselessness, unambiguously \"failure\").\n  - θ_stab(W) = pre-registered per bit, with defaults θ_stab(4)=1.0, θ_stab(3)=2.0, θ_stab(2)=5.0 ΔPPL (rationale: graded tolerance acknowledging less headroom at lower bits; \"acceptable\" ≈ under ~10–15% relative PPL inflation at 4-bit).\n  - **Claim S_W holds** iff, at bit W, for both quantizers: unrotated cell ΔPPL ≥ θ_collapse AND rotated cell ΔPPL ≤ θ_stab(W). **Claim G_W holds** iff rotated beats unrotated in ΔPPL with 95% CI excluding a 0.1-PPL equivalence margin. The research question's strong form (\"stabilizes where RTN/GPTQ fail\") = S_2 ∧ S_3; weak form = G_2 ∨ G_3.\n- **Mediation check:** test the causal chain rotation → outlier statistics (M1) → quantization error (M2) → ΔPPL. Practically: (i) rotation must significantly reduce per-group max/RMS and kurtosis (M1); (ii) ε_rel (M2) must be lower in rotated cells; (iii) adding per-layer mean M2 to the ΔPPL regression must substantially attenuate the rotation coefficient; model M1→M2→ΔPPL path coefficients.\n  - **Null interpretation:** if ΔPPL improves but M1/M2 don't move → the gain is not via the incoherence mechanism; look for confounds (e.g., implicit regularization through coverage differences) — attribution fails even if performance holds.\n  - **Dissociation interpretation:** if M1 improves but ΔPPL doesn't (esp. at W=2) → outlier flattening is necessary but insufficient; residual failure modes dominate (e.g., un-rotatable layers, embedding/loss-layer sensitivity), and the mechanism story must be scoped down. Either outcome is informative by construction.\n- **Secondary analyses:** rotation-marginal trajectory across W (expect widening gap toward 2-bit); GPTQ-vs-rotation interaction (expect subadditivity); R2−R1 gap quantifying the price of strict weight-only placement; R1x reported descriptively only.\n\n## 6. Feasibility (honestly scoped — nothing here was run)\n\nThis environment cannot execute the design (no GPU, no ML libraries, no datasets, no checkpoints, no network). A real run requires: 1× modern GPU (≥48 GB sufficient for ≤13B; ≥2×80 GB or offloading for 70B), HuggingFace BF16 checkpoints, an evaluation harness (lm-eval-harness class) and C4/WikiText-2, plus a GPTQ implementation and a fast Walsh–Hadamard transform. Rough cost drivers (order-of-magnitude planning numbers, not measurements): quantization itself is minor (GPTQ on 7–13B with 512-seq calibration: ~tens of minutes to a few hours per run; RTN: minutes; 108 primary runs ≈ low hundreds of GPU-hours dominated by eval); full perplexity + 7-task zero-shot eval per model-bit-seed cell is the dominant cost, amortized by sharing evals across cells that don't change the eval. **Graceful-demotion order if compute is tight:** (1) drop 70B extension; (2) drop R2 and R-sign/coverage ablations to W=2 only; (3) drop downstream suite to {LAMBADA, HellaSwag, MMLU} (perplexity, not accuracy, is the primary endpoint); (4) reduce seeds 3→2 and calibration sensitivity grid; (5) drop the third model to 2; (6) group sweep to {128, 32}. **Never drop:** the 12-cell 2×2×bits core, the equivalence audit, the R0 outlier-treatment arms at W=2, and the mechanistic measures — without them the attribution logic collapses.\n\n## 7. Biggest single threat to validity (flagged, not hidden)\n\n**Scale-dependent generalization.** The phenomenon QuaRot targets — activation/weight outliers whose severity grows with model scale and depth — is exactly the effect most likely to be *understated* at 7–13B, the scales this design can actually afford. If RTN/GPTQ degrade gracefully at 7B (where extra bits have more headroom) the S_2/S_3 pass/fail criterion could fail for scale reasons rather than mechanism reasons, and conversely a rotation win at 13B would not, by itself, license claims about the 70B-class regime where the original claims live. Mitigations are partial only: within-family two-scale contrast, optional 70B arm, and mechanistic measures (M1 outlier statistics) whose *trend with scale* is more transferable than the ΔPPL endpoint. A strong second threat: rotation×group-size interaction — if the default g is far from the regime where baselines are strongest, the rotation effect size is g-contingent; the co-variation sweep bounds but does not eliminate this.\n\n## 8. Assumptions stated separately from fixed requirements\n\nFixed requirements: F1–F5 (researcher-supplied). Design assumptions on top: (i) WikiText-2/C4 PPL and the standard zero-shot suite are adequate proxies for \"quality\" as the question intends; (ii) Llama-family as primary is representative enough given the external-validity block; (iii) symmetric uniform quantization is the intended default for both quantizers; (iv) \"weight-only\" is interpreted as F1 (activations always BF16; R2's runtime Hadamards touch activations numerically but quantize nothing but weights — hence quarantined as contextual); (v) all defaults marked UNVERIFIED are placeholders pending the actual setup_5/7/11/16/17 text, and the design's conclusions are intended to be robust to the sensitivity sweeps precisely because those defaults could not be sourced.", "evidence": "Design-only basis; supporting repository/environment observations: (1) /workspace contains only task.json (question + track.md text \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\") — verified in turn 1; no repos, QuaRot/RTN/GPTQ code, datasets, or checkpoints anywhere on the filesystem (find results, turn 1). (2) No executable ML stack (no torch/numpy/scipy/transformers/lm-eval; imports fail; network disabled; gpu_enabled=false) — hence §6 scopes execution to an external GPU environment and no numbers are presented as results. (3) Mount path metadata confirms NeurIPS 2024 paper 94328 (QuaRot) as source; paper text absent locally, which is why §0/F4 keeps group size, calibration size, coverage, and sign-flips as parameterized UNVERIFIED knobs rather than sourced facts. (4) RTN calibration-free handling in the 2×2 core follows researcher-supplied setup_16/17 definitions: RTN cells use zero data (rotation pre-modifies weights only), GPTQ cells share a byte-identical calibration set between R0/R1, so rotation is the sole within-quantizer delta. (5) Pass/fail thresholds (§5) are declared as pre-registered parameters with magnitude rationale (single-digit BF16 PPL regime for Llama-class models), not attributed to the source.", "assumptions": ["WikiText-2/C4 perplexity plus standard zero-shot suite are adequate 'quality' proxies for the question", "Llama-family 7-13B primary models are representative given one external-family block; conclusions about larger scales are extrapolations", "Symmetric uniform grids are the intended default for RTN and GPTQ", "Weight-only per F1 means no activation/KV quantization anywhere; R2's fused/online BF16 Hadamards are contextual only", "All parameterized defaults (g=128, 512x2048 calibration, offline-full coverage, sign-flips on) are placeholders UNVERIFIED against setup topics 5/7/11/16/17, with sensitivity checks provided"], "uncertainties": ["True contents of setup topics 5/7/11/16/17 remain unavailable to both parties; hyperparameters are parameterized rather than sourced", "Which GPTQ variant (uniform vs non-uniform grid) the source comparisons used — exploratory arm flagged, not primary", "Scale-dependence of the effect: 7-13B findings may understate or misstate the 70B+ regime (flagged as the biggest single validity threat in §7)", "Rotation x group-size interaction could make the measured rotation effect size group-size contingent; bounded by the co-variation sweep but not eliminated"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9dd9ed5eac367d87", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-9dd9ed5eac367d87", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Final design: 2x2 quantizer x Hadamard rotation, weight-only W4/3/2\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment Design: Isolating the Effect of QuaRot's Hadamard Transformation on Weight-Only Low-Bit Quantization (W \\u2208 {4,3,2}) vs. RTN and GPTQ\\n\\n**Plain statement up front:** No experiment was run. Every number below is either a pre-registered design parameter (with rationale) or an illustrative order-of-magnitude cost estimate. Nothing here is a measured result. The environment (/workspace: task.json only; no repos, no torch/numpy, no datasets, no GPU, no network) supports design only, and this deliverable is design only.\\n\\n## 0. Fixed constraints (from researcher brief, not assumptions)\\n\\n- **F1.** Weight-only quantization: only model weights are quantized. W4A4KV4 activation/KV arms are **out of scope as primary results** (setup_5, setup_7).\\n- **F2.** W \\u2208 {4, 3, 2} (setup_11); BF16 appears only as a normalization anchor, never as a bit-width cell.\\n- **F3.** RTN = round-to-nearest, **calibration-free** (setup_16). GPTQ = error-minimizing quantization via Hessian approximation on calibration data (setup_17).\\n- **F4.** Group size, calibration size, rotation layer coverage, and sign-flip usage are **parameterized knobs with defaults, marked UNVERIFIED against the paper** \\u2014 neither party has the source text locally; no hyperparameter below is attributed to the paper as fact.\\n- **F5.** \\\"Collapse\\\" and \\\"acceptable degradation\\\" thresholds are pre-registered parameters with stated rationale, not sourced numbers.\\n\\n## 1. Rationale \\u2014 the 2\\u00d72 quantizer \\u00d7 rotation core\\n\\nThe question conflates two things: (a) QuaRot-the-pipeline beating RTN/GPTQ-the-pipelines, and (b) whether the Hadamard **rotation itself** is the causal ingredient that stabilizes low-bit weight quantization. (a) is confounded \\u2014 the pipeline differs in granularity, outlier handling, and coverage simultaneously. So the design's primary comparison is a **2\\u00d72 factorial held fixed on every other dimension**:\\n\\n|  | Rotation: OFF | Rotation: ON (offline-equivalent) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **RTN** (calibration-free) | C1 | C2 |\\n| **GPTQ** (Hessian-based) | C3 | C4 |\\n\\n\\u00d7 bit-width W \\u2208 {4,3,2} = **12 primary cells per (model, group size, seed)**.\\n\\nMechanistic hypothesis being tested: Hadamard rotation multiplies weight/activation distributions by a structured orthogonal matrix, spreading energy across channels (incoherence reduction), which shrinks the dynamic range *within each quantization group*, thereby reducing rounding error \\u2016WX \\u2212 Q(W)X\\u2016 at fixed bit budget. The 2\\u00d72 core attributes any \\u0394 to rotation because, within each quantizer row: identical grid, rounding rule, group size, layer coverage, seeds, and (for GPTQ) identical calibration set \\u2014 **rotation is the only thing varied**. For RTN, no calibration data enters either RTN cell (the rotated weights are rounded without data; rotation modifies the pre-rounding weights, not the algorithm's data access), so RTN remains calibration-free as defined by F3.\\n\\nSecondary contrasts the same grid yields:\\n- **C4 \\u2212 C2** = does GPTQ's error compensation still help after rotation removes the error structure it compensates for? (Predicted: interaction \\u2014 rotation's marginal gain shrinks as quantizer sophistication grows, and grows as W shrinks.)\\n- **Gain(W) slope**: is the rotation effect largest at W=2, where RTN/GPTQ are expected to fail? This operationalizes the second half of the research question.\\n\\n## 2. Arms, knobs, and full grid\\n\\n### 2.1 Rotation arms (Factor C)\\n\\n- **R0 \\u2014 none.** Raw BF16 weights \\u2192 quantizer.\\n- **R1 \\u2014 offline-equivalent rotation (PRIMARY \\\"ON\\\" arm).** Mathematically equivalent transformation implemented entirely in the weights: fuse H into LayerNorm weights and across residual streams (X = XHH\\u1d40 trick) at block boundaries with a fixed seed; rotate offline the input channels of every linear layer whose input is covered by the propagated rotation (embeddings, QKV projections, MLP up/gate projections), W \\u2190 WH. Activations stay BF16 at all times. **Coverage of the offline chain is a knob K_cov \\u2208 {full-linear-subset (default), attention-only, MLP-only} (UNVERIFIED default).**\\n- **R1x \\u2014 uncompensated weight-column-only rotation (NON-EQUIVALENT control arm).** Multiply each linear weight's input columns by H with no compensation anywhere. This deliberately changes the function; it is labeled non-equivalent and reported separately. Purpose: test whether naive \\\"rotate the weights\\\" (function-breaking) trivially mimics the gain, and quantify what the equivalence machinery buys.\\n- **R2 \\u2014 full QuaRot-equivalent placement including online Hadamards (EQUIVALENCE-REFERENCE / CONTEXTUAL arm, labeled non-primary per F1).** Adds online (runtime-activation, still BF16) Hadamards so that inputs of attention out-proj and MLP down-proj are also rotation-covered. Kept out of the primary ANOVA (it changes runtime compute and violates strict weight-only interpretation of \\\"nothing but weights changes\\\"); used only to bound how much of the effect is unreachable under strict weight-only placement.\\n- **R-sign \\u2014 sign-flip ablation knob K_sign \\u2208 {random diagonal sign flip ON (default), OFF} (UNVERIFIED default)**, applied within R1. Tests whether randomizing sign flips (breaking inter-block Hadamard alignment) matters.\\n\\n### 2.2 Quantizer cells\\n\\n- **RTN:** symmetric uniform grid, min/max per group (or per-channel for g=\\u221e), no data used.\\n- **GPTQ:** Hessian approximation from calibration forward passes, Cholesky-ordered column-wise error compensation, same grid/group size as paired RTN cell, identical hyperparameters (block size, damping 1% default) across rotation states.\\n- Optional exploratory (not primary): GPTQ with non-uniform grid \\u2014 flagged because the paper's exact GPTQ variant is unknown (uncertainty preserved, not guessed).\\n\\n### 2.3 Parameterized knobs (defaults are design choices, not paper claims)\\n\\n| Knob | Default | Sensitivity check | Status |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Group size g | 128 | {32, 64, 128, per-channel(g=\\u221e)} | UNVERIFIED |\\n| Calibration set | C4-style, 512 \\u00d7 2048-token seqs, seed s_C | {128, 512, 1024} seqs; 2 draws of s_C | UNVERIFIED |\\n| Rotation coverage K_cov | offline-full | ablations above | UNVERIFIED |\\n| Sign flips K_sign | ON | OFF | UNVERIFIED |\\n| Quant seeds | 3 | \\u2014 | design choice |\\n\\n### 2.4 Alternative outlier-treatment arms (Factor E) \\u2014 same bit budget, no Hadamard\\n\\nAt each W, paired with R0: (a) **per-channel scaling / SmoothQuant-style migration** adapted to weight-only (per-channel max-rescaling); (b) **mixed-precision outlier keeping**: top-0.1% highest-|w| channels per layer kept BF16 (matched to the approximate extra-bit budget of small groups). These isolate \\\"Hadamard incoherence\\\" from generic outlier mitigation, so the design cannot accidentally credit rotation with benefits any range-reduction trick would give.\\n\\n### 2.5 Models (Factor F), blocked by family\\n\\n- **Primary:** Llama-family 7\\u20138B and 13B (two scales within one family \\u2192 outlier severity and quantization tolerance vary with scale/depth).\\n- **External-validity block:** one architecturally different family at ~7B (e.g., Mistral-class or Falcon-class; specific choice deferred to checkpoints available at run time).\\n- **Optional scale extension (clearly marked, drop-first):** 70B-class.\\n- BF16 anchor per model for \\u0394 normalization (F2).\\n\\n### 2.6 Full grid size (primary + secondary), for feasibility math\\n\\nPrimary: 2 quantizers \\u00d7 2 rotation \\u00d7 3 bits \\u00d7 g=128 \\u00d7 3 models \\u00d7 3 seeds = **108 quantization runs** (each cheap relative to eval). Secondary arms (R1x, R2, sign-off, coverage, outlier treatments, group sweep, calibration sensitivity) \\u2248 **+70\\u201390 runs**, most at W \\u2208 {2,3} only, using a fractional-factorial subset rather than full crossing.\\n\\n## 3. Metrics\\n\\n- **Primary endpoint:** \\u0394PPL = PPL(cell) \\u2212 PPL(BF16 anchor) on WikiText-2 (fixed evaluation protocol: 2048-token contiguous, non-overlapping). Secondary: C4-validation PPL.\\n- **Downstream:** zero-shot suite (PIQA, LAMBADA, ARC-e/c, Winogrande, HellaSwag, MMLU) \\u2014 reported as mean-normalized accuracy vs. BF16. Chosen because at W=2 PPL is a continuous, non-saturating measure where accuracy metrics floor at chance and lose resolution.\\n- **Mechanistic/mediation measures (this is what makes the design explanatory, not just comparative):**\\n  - M1: per-layer input-channel kurtosis and max/RMS ratio of weights, pre- vs post-rotation (does H flatten the distribution as theory predicts?).\\n  - M2: relative quantization error on held-out calibration inputs, \\u03b5_rel = \\u2016WX \\u2212 Q(W)X\\u2016_F/\\u2016WX\\u2016_F per layer, in the same forward pass structure as each cell.\\n  - M3: activation outlier statistics (max-activation fraction) \\u2014 reported for context even though activations are not quantized (relates R1 vs R2 gap to residual activation outliers).\\n- **Overhead accounting:** rotation/quantization wall-clock; since activations remain BF16 and R1 adds zero inference-time ops (fully fused), runtime cost is identical across R0/R1 cells \\u2014 an important \\\"free lunch\\\" audit.\\n\\n## 4. Controls and anti-confound logic (consolidated)\\n\\n1. **Rotation-is-the-only-delta within quantizer rows** \\u2014 grids, group size, rounding, damping, layer coverage, seeds, and GPTQ calibration set byte-identical between R0/R1 cells. RTN uses no data in either row (F3).\\n2. **Group-size co-variation** \\u2014 the g \\u2208 {32,64,128,\\u221e} sweep is crossed with \\u00b1rotation at W \\u2208 {2,3}; the rotation effect is estimated *within* each (quantizer, W, g) cell, so a rotation win cannot be a grouping artifact, and a \\\"grouping explains it\\\" critique is directly testable.\\n3. **Alternative outlier treatments at matched bit budget** (\\u00a72.4) \\u2014 separates Hadamard incoherence from generic range reduction.\\n4. **Equivalence audit** \\u2014 assert PPL(R1, BF16 weights unquantized, rotations applied) within \\u00b10.01 of BF16 anchor before any quantized cell is trusted; R1x is explicitly exempt (it must fail this audit \\u2014 that's its purpose).\\n5. **Family/scale blocking** \\u2014 model entered as a blocking factor; all cells run within each model, so family effects cannot masquerade as rotation effects.\\n6. **Calibration and seed sensitivity** \\u2014 rotation contrast re-estimated at |C| \\u2208 {128,1024} and across 3 quant seeds and 2 calibration draws; RTN (data-free) doubles as a check that seed/reporting variance from data is being attributed to GPTQ only.\\n7. **Non-primary arms quarantined** \\u2014 R2 results are labeled contextual and excluded from the primary model, honoring F1.\\n\\n## 5. Analysis plan\\n\\n- **Primary endpoint:** \\u0394PPL_W2 for the second question; full \\u0394PPL surface for the first.\\n- **Model:** mixed-effects regression\\n  \\u0394PPL ~ quantizer \\u00d7 rotation \\u00d7 bits + (bits as factor) + g + (1 | model) + (1 | seed),\\n  fitted on the primary 108 cells; planned contrasts (Tukey-adjusted): C2\\u2212C1, C4\\u2212C3, and interaction quantizer\\u00d7rotation at each W. Family-blocking via model random effects; sensitivity refit with model as fixed effect.\\n- **Pre-registered pass/fail criterion operationalizing \\\"stabilizes at 2\\u20133 bit where RTN/GPTQ fail.\\\"** Two thresholds, declared before any run (F5 rationale, not sourced):\\n  - \\u03b8_collapse = 10.0 \\u0394PPL (rationale: Llama-class 7\\u201313B BF16 WikiText-2 PPL is single-digit; +10 is a >2\\u00d7 degradation and near-task-uselessness, unambiguously \\\"failure\\\").\\n  - \\u03b8_stab(W) = pre-registered per bit, with defaults \\u03b8_stab(4)=1.0, \\u03b8_stab(3)=2.0, \\u03b8_stab(2)=5.0 \\u0394PPL (rationale: graded tolerance acknowledging less headroom at lower bits; \\\"acceptable\\\" \\u2248 under ~10\\u201315% relative PPL inflation at 4-bit).\\n  - **Claim S_W holds** iff, at bit W, for both quantizers: unrotated cell \\u0394PPL \\u2265 \\u03b8_collapse AND rotated cell \\u0394PPL \\u2264 \\u03b8_stab(W). **Claim G_W holds** iff rotated beats unrotated in \\u0394PPL with 95% CI excluding a 0.1-PPL equivalence margin. The research question's strong form (\\\"stabilizes where RTN/GPTQ fail\\\") = S_2 \\u2227 S_3; weak form = G_2 \\u2228 G_3.\\n- **Mediation check:** test the causal chain rotation \\u2192 outlier statistics (M1) \\u2192 quantization error (M2) \\u2192 \\u0394PPL. Practically: (i) rotation must significantly reduce per-group max/RMS and kurtosis (M1); (ii) \\u03b5_rel (M2) must be lower in rotated cells; (iii) adding per-layer mean M2 to the \\u0394PPL regression must substantially attenuate the rotation coefficient; model M1\\u2192M2\\u2192\\u0394PPL path coefficients.\\n  - **Null interpretation:** if \\u0394PPL improves but M1/M2 don't move \\u2192 the gain is not via the incoherence mechanism; look for confounds (e.g., implicit regularization through coverage differences) \\u2014 attribution fails even if performance holds.\\n  - **Dissociation interpretation:** if M1 improves but \\u0394PPL doesn't (esp. at W=2) \\u2192 outlier flattening is necessary but insufficient; residual failure modes dominate (e.g., un-rotatable layers, embedding/loss-layer sensitivity), and the mechanism story must be scoped down. Either outcome is informative by construction.\\n- **Secondary analyses:** rotation-marginal trajectory across W (expect widening gap toward 2-bit); GPTQ-vs-rotation interaction (expect subadditivity); R2\\u2212R1 gap quantifying the price of strict weight-only placement; R1x reported descriptively only.\\n\\n## 6. Feasibility (honestly scoped \\u2014 nothing here was run)\\n\\nThis environment cannot execute the design (no GPU, no ML libraries, no datasets, no checkpoints, no network). A real run requires: 1\\u00d7 modern GPU (\\u226548 GB sufficient for \\u226413B; \\u22652\\u00d780 GB or offloading for 70B), HuggingFace BF16 checkpoints, an evaluation harness (lm-eval-harness class) and C4/WikiText-2, plus a GPTQ implementation and a fast Walsh\\u2013Hadamard transform. Rough cost drivers (order-of-magnitude planning numbers, not measurements): quantization itself is minor (GPTQ on 7\\u201313B with 512-seq calibration: ~tens of minutes to a few hours per run; RTN: minutes; 108 primary runs \\u2248 low hundreds of GPU-hours dominated by eval); full perplexity + 7-task zero-shot eval per model-bit-seed cell is the dominant cost, amortized by sharing evals across cells that don't change the eval. **Graceful-demotion order if compute is tight:** (1) drop 70B extension; (2) drop R2 and R-sign/coverage ablations to W=2 only; (3) drop downstream suite to {LAMBADA, HellaSwag, MMLU} (perplexity, not accuracy, is the primary endpoint); (4) reduce seeds 3\\u21922 and calibration sensitivity grid; (5) drop the third model to 2; (6) group sweep to {128, 32}. **Never drop:** the 12-cell 2\\u00d72\\u00d7bits core, the equivalence audit, the R0 outlier-treatment arms at W=2, and the mechanistic measures \\u2014 without them the attribution logic collapses.\\n\\n## 7. Biggest single threat to validity (flagged, not hidden)\\n\\n**Scale-dependent generalization.** The phenomenon QuaRot targets \\u2014 activation/weight outliers whose severity grows with model scale and depth \\u2014 is exactly the effect most likely to be *understated* at 7\\u201313B, the scales this design can actually afford. If RTN/GPTQ degrade gracefully at 7B (where extra bits have more headroom) the S_2/S_3 pass/fail criterion could fail for scale reasons rather than mechanism reasons, and conversely a rotation win at 13B would not, by itself, license claims about the 70B-class regime where the original claims live. Mitigations are partial only: within-family two-scale contrast, optional 70B arm, and mechanistic measures (M1 outlier statistics) whose *trend with scale* is more transferable than the \\u0394PPL endpoint. A strong second threat: rotation\\u00d7group-size interaction \\u2014 if the default g is far from the regime where baselines are strongest, the rotation effect size is g-contingent; the co-variation sweep bounds but does not eliminate this.\\n\\n## 8. Assumptions stated separately from fixed requirements\\n\\nFixed requirements: F1\\u2013F5 (researcher-supplied). Design assumptions on top: (i) WikiText-2/C4 PPL and the standard zero-shot suite are adequate proxies for \\\"quality\\\" as the question intends; (ii) Llama-family as primary is representative enough given the external-validity block; (iii) symmetric uniform quantization is the intended default for both quantizers; (iv) \\\"weight-only\\\" is interpreted as F1 (activations always BF16; R2's runtime Hadamards touch activations numerically but quantize nothing but weights \\u2014 hence quarantined as contextual); (v) all defaults marked UNVERIFIED are placeholders pending the actual setup_5/7/11/16/17 text, and the design's conclusions are intended to be robust to the sensitivity sweeps precisely because those defaults could not be sourced.\", \"evidence\": \"Design-only basis; supporting repository/environment observations: (1) /workspace contains only task.json (question + track.md text \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\") \\u2014 verified in turn 1; no repos, QuaRot/RTN/GPTQ code, datasets, or checkpoints anywhere on the filesystem (find results, turn 1). (2) No executable ML stack (no torch/numpy/scipy/transformers/lm-eval; imports fail; network disabled; gpu_enabled=false) \\u2014 hence \\u00a76 scopes execution to an external GPU environment and no numbers are presented as results. (3) Mount path metadata confirms NeurIPS 2024 paper 94328 (QuaRot) as source; paper text absent locally, which is why \\u00a70/F4 keeps group size, calibration size, coverage, and sign-flips as parameterized UNVERIFIED knobs rather than sourced facts. (4) RTN calibration-free handling in the 2\\u00d72 core follows researcher-supplied setup_16/17 definitions: RTN cells use zero data (rotation pre-modifies weights only), GPTQ cells share a byte-identical calibration set between R0/R1, so rotation is the sole within-quantizer delta. (5) Pass/fail thresholds (\\u00a75) are declared as pre-registered parameters with magnitude rationale (single-digit BF16 PPL regime for Llama-class models), not attributed to the source.\", \"assumptions\": [\"WikiText-2/C4 perplexity plus standard zero-shot suite are adequate 'quality' proxies for the question\", \"Llama-family 7-13B primary models are representative given one external-family block; conclusions about larger scales are extrapolations\", \"Symmetric uniform grids are the intended default for RTN and GPTQ\", \"Weight-only per F1 means no activation/KV quantization anywhere; R2's fused/online BF16 Hadamards are contextual only\", \"All parameterized defaults (g=128, 512x2048 calibration, offline-full coverage, sign-flips on) are placeholders UNVERIFIED against setup topics 5/7/11/16/17, with sensitivity checks provided\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"True contents of setup topics 5/7/11/16/17 remain unavailable to both parties; hyperparameters are parameterized rather than sourced\", \"Which GPTQ variant (uniform vs non-uniform grid) the source comparisons used \\u2014 exploratory arm flagged, not primary\", \"Scale-dependence of the effect: 7-13B findings may understate or misstate the 70B+ regime (flagged as the biggest single validity threat in \\u00a77)\", \"Rotation x group-size interaction could make the measured rotation effect size group-size contingent; bounded by the co-variation sweep but not eliminated\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-e2b29d16-1bdf-4772-b8ba-4ecd768f2f5d", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Final design: 2x2 quantizer x Hadamard rotation, weight-only W4/3/2", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
